Picture two teams, each shipping a `Card` component, each writing plain CSS:

```css
/* team A, checkout/Card.css */
.card { padding: 16px; border-radius: 8px; }

/* team B, dashboard/Card.css, loaded on the same page */
.card { padding: 24px; box-shadow: 0 2px 4px rgba(0,0,0,.1); }
```

Both files load on any page that imports both features. Whichever loads later wins on the shared `padding` declaration — not by design, by [source order](css-cascade-and-specificity), an accident of import sequence. Neither team did anything wrong in isolation; the collision only exists because CSS has no concept of "this class belongs to this component and nothing else."

At five components this is a one-off bug. At five hundred components across a dozen teams, it's a recurring source of "I changed a class name and something unrelated on another page broke" incidents — which is exactly the failure mode all four approaches below are built to eliminate.

CSS Modules make scoping automatic rather than a naming convention someone has to remember. You write a normal-looking class name; the build tool rewrites it to something unique before it ever reaches the browser:

```css
/* Card.module.css */
.card { padding: 16px; }
```
```jsx
import styles from './Card.module.css';
// styles.card === "Card_card__a3f9x" (exact hash format varies by tooling)
<div className={styles.card}>...</div>
```

The generated class name bakes in the file (and often the component) it came from, so two `.card` classes in two different modules produce two different runtime class names — collisions become structurally impossible rather than merely unlikely, closing the gap BEM leaves open. The cost: it's a build step, so it doesn't work without bundler support, and the generated class names are meaningless in devtools without the source map connecting them back — debugging "which file did this style come from" is a step removed from just reading the class name.

CSS-in-JS libraries (styled-components, Emotion, and similar) write the styles inside the component file itself, generating scoped class names the same way CSS Modules do, but with the added ability to use JS values — props, theme tokens, computed state — directly inside the style:

```jsx
const Card = styled.div`
  padding: 16px;
  background: ${props => props.featured ? '#fff8e1' : '#fff'};
`;

<Card featured={isFeatured}>...</Card>
```

This solves a real problem the previous two approaches don't: dynamic, prop-driven styling without reaching for a separate class-toggling dance (`className={isFeatured ? 'card featured' : 'card'}`). The trade-off is runtime cost for the classic implementations — style computation and injection happen in the browser on render, which is measurably slower than a static stylesheet the browser can parse once. Newer "zero-runtime" CSS-in-JS variants extract styles at build time specifically to claw that cost back, at the price of losing some of the fully-dynamic flexibility.
